---
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import WidgetWrapper from '~/components/ui/WidgetWrapper.astro';

// The second mode, in the same shape as OverlayFacets — four blocks, alternating, each with
// one artefact beside it. Deliberate: Inspect and Govern are one panel, so the two sections
// must not look like two products.
//
// This replaced a Prototype section: pick a component off your Storybook, place a control
// that does not exist yet, hand a developer an address instead of a ticket. It demoed well
// and it was the wrong thing to spend the second half of a product page on — that is a
// crowded space with better-funded entrants, and it argued for a feature rather than for
// what this product is.
//
// What is here instead is the composition, which is the actual claim: five controls, one
// order, and the choice of how much of it you take. Inspect answers questions about a
// control; Govern is where you change the answers, from the same panel, without a deploy.
//
// The control under discussion is the same Refund button the hero governs, so a reader
// arrives already knowing the screen, the address and whose seat it is.

// The chain, outermost first. This is the one list on the site that has to stay in this
// order — it is the order the controls run, and the argument for the order is in the copy
// beside it. Rendered top to bottom with the handler at the foot, which is how a middleware
// stack and a provider tree both read.
const chain = [
  {
    name: 'Error logs',
    icon: 'tabler:alert-triangle',
    role: 'Wraps everything, including the controls themselves',
    reader: 'Engineering',
  },
  {
    name: 'User analytics',
    icon: 'tabler:chart-line',
    role: 'Opens on the attempt, closes on the outcome',
    reader: 'Product',
  },
  {
    name: 'Audit logs',
    icon: 'tabler:history',
    role: 'Records the intent, sealed with what happened',
    reader: 'Compliance',
  },
  { name: 'Feature flags', icon: 'tabler:toggle-right', role: 'Does this exist for you at all?', reader: 'Release' },
  { name: 'Access controls', icon: 'tabler:lock', role: 'May you?', reader: 'Security' },
];
---

<WidgetWrapper containerClass="max-w-5xl mx-auto">
  <div class="text-center mb-14">
    <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-primary mb-4">Five controls, one order</p>
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight">
      Take one control, or all five.<br class="hidden sm:block" /> On one button, or on all of them.
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-5 text-muted max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
      The panel that reads your controls is also where you change them. Add a flag to a single button, or wrap every
      control in your product in all five at once — and either way you write it from inside the running product rather
      than from a console that has never rendered it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="space-y-16 sm:space-y-20">
    <!-- ── 1. Composition ───────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 md:gap-12 items-center">
      <div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2.5 mb-3">
          <Icon name="tabler:stack-2" class="w-5 h-5 text-primary" />
          <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-primary">01 · Compose it</span>
        </div>
        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Nothing is all-or-nothing.</h3>
        <p class="text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
          A provider at the root takes the controls you want everywhere. A wrapper on one control takes the ones you
          want just there. A team that only wants feature flags on one button installs feature flags on one button, and
          nothing else changes.
        </p>
        <p class="text-muted leading-relaxed">
          Which means the first install is never a migration. It is one control, one flag, and a coverage number that
          was zero and is now one.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-slate-900 overflow-hidden shadow-xl">
        <div class="px-3 py-2 border-b border-white/8 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-slate-400">Your app</span>
          <span class="ml-auto text-[10px] font-mono text-slate-500">App.tsx</span>
        </div>
        <pre
          class="p-3.5 text-[11px] leading-relaxed font-mono text-slate-300 overflow-x-auto"><code><span class="text-slate-600">{'// everywhere'}</span>
{'<'}<span class="text-accent">DnaProvider</span> <span class="text-slate-400">controls</span>={'{['}<span class="text-emerald-300">'flags'</span>, <span class="text-emerald-300">'access'</span>, <span class="text-emerald-300">'audit'</span>{']}'}{'>'}

  <span class="text-slate-600">{'// ...or just here'}</span>
  {'<'}<span class="text-accent">Dna.Control</span> <span class="text-slate-400">op</span>=<span class="text-emerald-300">"order.refund"</span> <span class="text-slate-400">flag</span>=<span class="text-emerald-300">"instant-refunds"</span>{'>'}
    {'<'}<span class="text-sky-300">RefundButton</span> {'/>'}
  {'</'}<span class="text-accent">Dna.Control</span>{'>'}

{'</'}<span class="text-accent">DnaProvider</span>{'>'}</code></pre>
        <p class="px-3.5 pb-3 text-[10px] text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
          The same five names on the API and the agent, so a team moves between surfaces without relearning anything.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── 2. The order ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 md:gap-12 items-center">
      <div class="md:order-last">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2.5 mb-3">
          <Icon name="tabler:arrow-down" class="w-5 h-5 text-primary" />
          <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-primary">02 · The order is the design</span>
        </div>
        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Ask the flag before you ask the rule.</h3>
        <p class="text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
          A control that is off for you is <em>absent</em>, not refused — so the flag runs first, and nothing under it
          runs at all. That is a security property before it is a performance one: a 403 tells somebody the feature
          exists and is not theirs, and off tells them nothing, because there is nothing to tell.
        </p>
        <p class="text-muted leading-relaxed">
          The three recording controls wrap the two deciding ones, which is the only arrangement where a refusal is
          itself auditable and a failure <em>inside</em> the checks still gets caught.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-slate-900 overflow-hidden shadow-xl">
        <div class="px-3 py-2 border-b border-white/8 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-[11px] font-semibold text-white border border-primary/60 rounded px-2 py-0.5">Govern</span>
          <span class="ml-auto text-[10px] font-mono text-slate-500">order.refund</span>
        </div>
        <div class="p-3.5">
          {
            chain.map((c, i) => (
              <div
                class="rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-slate-800/40 px-2.5 py-2"
                style={`margin-left:${i * 10}px`}
              >
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
                  <Icon name={c.icon} class="w-3.5 h-3.5 flex-shrink-0 text-primary" />
                  <span class="text-[11px] text-slate-200 font-medium">{c.name}</span>
                  <span class="ml-auto text-[9px] uppercase tracking-wider text-slate-500 whitespace-nowrap">
                    {c.reader}
                  </span>
                </div>
                <p class="mt-0.5 text-[10px] text-slate-500 leading-snug">{c.role}</p>
              </div>
            ))
          }
          <div
            class="mt-1.5 rounded-lg border border-primary/40 bg-primary/10 px-2.5 py-2 flex items-center gap-2"
            style="margin-left:50px"
          >
            <Icon name="tabler:pointer" class="w-3.5 h-3.5 flex-shrink-0 text-accent" />
            <span class="text-[11px] text-slate-100 font-medium">Your control</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── 3. Writing it, from inside ───────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 md:gap-12 items-center">
      <div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2.5 mb-3">
          <Icon name="tabler:pencil" class="w-5 h-5 text-primary" />
          <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-primary">03 · Change it where you are</span>
        </div>
        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">No deploy, and no second tab.</h3>
        <p class="text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Restrict an act to a seat, flip a flag, detach a rule — standing on the control it affects, in staging or in
          production. Nobody has to find <code class="text-accent text-[0.9em]">refund_v2_enabled</code> in a list and hope
          it still means this button.
        </p>
        <p class="text-muted leading-relaxed">
          The subject is the <em>act</em>, not the button. Write the rule once and every control that refunds an order
          is holding it, including the ones you have not found yet.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-slate-900 overflow-hidden shadow-xl">
        <div class="px-3 py-2 border-b border-white/8 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-[11px] font-semibold text-white border border-primary/60 rounded px-2 py-0.5">Govern</span>
          <span class="ml-auto text-[10px] font-mono text-slate-500">order.refund</span>
        </div>
        <div class="p-3.5">
          <p class="text-[9px] uppercase tracking-wider text-slate-500 mb-1.5">Restrict to</p>
          <div class="space-y-1">
            {
              [
                { seat: 'Fulfillment desk', on: true },
                { seat: 'Store manager', on: true },
                { seat: 'Support', on: false },
              ].map((r) => (
                <div class="flex items-center gap-2 rounded bg-slate-800/50 px-2 py-1.5">
                  <span class="text-[11px] text-slate-300">{r.seat}</span>
                  <span class="ml-auto inline-flex items-center gap-1">
                    <span
                      class:list={[
                        'text-[9px] uppercase tracking-wider font-bold',
                        r.on ? 'text-accent' : 'text-slate-600',
                      ]}
                    >
                      {r.on ? 'On' : 'Off'}
                    </span>
                    <span class:list={['w-6 h-3.5 rounded-full relative', r.on ? 'bg-primary/70' : 'bg-slate-700']}>
                      <span
                        class:list={[
                          'absolute top-0.5 w-2.5 h-2.5 rounded-full',
                          r.on ? 'right-0.5 bg-white' : 'left-0.5 bg-slate-400',
                        ]}
                      />
                    </span>
                  </span>
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </div>

          <div class="mt-3 rounded-lg border border-primary/30 bg-primary/8 px-3 py-2.5">
            <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-200 leading-relaxed">
              Written from here. No deploy, and every refusal from here on is recorded with it.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── 4. Authority ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 md:gap-12 items-center">
      <div class="md:order-last">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2.5 mb-3">
          <Icon name="tabler:gavel" class="w-5 h-5 text-primary" />
          <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-primary">04 · Who may change it</span>
        </div>
        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Writing a rule and making it decide are two grants.</h3>
        <p class="text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Anything written here arrives <strong class="text-white">proposed</strong> and governs nothing until somebody holding
          <code class="text-accent text-[0.9em]">ratify</code> puts it in force. So a panel that anyone on the team can open
          is not a panel anyone on the team can change your product with.
        </p>
        <p class="text-muted leading-relaxed">
          Which is also the answer to the obvious objection about a governance tool that lives in production: reading is
          free and unlimited, and authority is the thing we charge for and the thing we gate.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-slate-900 overflow-hidden shadow-xl">
        <div class="px-3 py-2 border-b border-white/8 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-[11px] font-semibold text-white border border-primary/60 rounded px-2 py-0.5">Govern</span>
          <span class="ml-auto text-[10px] text-slate-500">Pending · 1</span>
        </div>
        <div class="p-3.5">
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-amber-400/25 bg-amber-400/5 px-2.5 py-2">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="text-[11px] text-slate-200 font-medium">Restrict order.refund</span>
              <span
                class="ml-auto text-[8px] font-bold uppercase tracking-wider bg-amber-400/15 text-amber-300 rounded px-1 py-0.5"
                >proposed</span
              >
            </div>
            <p class="mt-1.5 text-[10px] text-slate-500">June Alcott · 2 minutes ago · governs nothing yet</p>
          </div>

          <ol class="mt-3 space-y-2.5">
            {
              [
                { state: 'Proposed', by: 'anybody who may read the panel', done: true },
                { state: 'In force', by: 'somebody holding ratify', done: false },
              ].map((row) => (
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <Icon
                    name={row.done ? 'tabler:circle-check-filled' : 'tabler:circle-dashed'}
                    class:list={['w-4 h-4 flex-shrink-0 mt-[1px]', row.done ? 'text-primary' : 'text-slate-600']}
                  />
                  <span class="text-[11px] leading-relaxed">
                    <strong class:list={[row.done ? 'text-slate-100' : 'text-slate-400']}>{row.state}</strong>
                    <span class="text-slate-500"> · {row.by}</span>
                  </span>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ol>

          <p class="mt-3 pt-3 border-t border-white/8 text-[10px] text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
            Every proposal, and every ratification, is itself an audited act.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- What it is not — said before anybody has to ask. -->
  <div class="mt-16 max-w-3xl mx-auto rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-slate-900/50 px-6 py-6 sm:px-8">
    <p class="text-lg font-bold text-white">You do not have to replace what already works.</p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">
      Start with the controls that have no governance at all, which is most of them, and leave your flag vendor and your
      roles table alone. Teams that later consolidate do it because they wanted one answer per control, not because we
      required it.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
      Reading is free and unlimited on every plan. Writing needs a seat — see the
      <a href="/pricing" class="text-primary hover:text-accent font-semibold">ladder</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</WidgetWrapper>
